WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.386
Welcome to our creative lab.

2
00:00:02.386 --> 00:00:07.159
Here, students take photographs, build animations, design T-shirts, explore 3D

3
00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:09.545
printing, and create virtual productions.

4
00:00:09.545 --> 00:00:15.000
Behind every project is learning we want to understand.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.860
We begin with a clear learning goal.

6
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.311
What message should this design communicate?

7
00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:21.946
Which techniques will help?

8
00:00:21.946 --> 00:00:27.000
Students know what successful learning looks like before we assess it.

9
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.568
P stands for product.

10
00:00:28.568 --> 00:00:34.056
A photograph, a digital model, or a finished animation shows how a student puts

11
00:00:34.056 --> 00:00:35.232
learning into practice.

12
00:00:35.232 --> 00:00:40.000
But the finished piece doesn't tell the whole story.

13
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.610
C stands for conversation.

14
00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.636
A short exchange can reveal the thinking behind a choice.

15
00:00:45.636 --> 00:00:47.649
Why make the lettering larger?

16
00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:50.870
How does this camera angle affect the audience?

17
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.000
We listen for understanding.

18
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.621
O stands for observation.

19
00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:59.888
We notice skills as they happen: adjusting light, testing a model, revising an

20
00:00:59.888 --> 00:01:01.914
edit, or using equipment safely.

21
00:01:01.914 --> 00:01:06.000
We connect what we see to the learning goal.

22
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.680
Teachers bring this evidence together over time and compare it with the criteria.

23
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.920
The mix can vary from one learner to another.

24
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:20.000
These are different ways to understand learning, not three equal slices of a grade.

25
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.495
In PowerSchool, look for P, C, or O at the beginning of an assessment title.

26
00:01:25.495 --> 00:01:28.792
That letter tells you where the evidence came from.

27
00:01:28.792 --> 00:01:33.187
The learning focus and feedback explain what your child demonstrated, and what

28
00:01:33.187 --> 00:01:35.000
comes next.

29
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.860
FORM means formative: feedback to help learning move forward.

30
00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:44.008
In our course format, these practice entries do not count toward the

31
00:01:44.008 --> 00:01:44.437
grade.

32
00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:47.868
SUM means summative: evidence used to evaluate achievement.

33
00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:51.729
Any of the three sources can serve either purpose.

34
00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:56.000
Summative doesn't have to mean the last attempt.

35
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.528
Feedback should lead somewhere.

36
00:01:57.528 --> 00:02:02.493
A student might test whether their design is readable from a distance, revise

37
00:02:02.493 --> 00:02:04.403
it, and explain what improved.

38
00:02:04.403 --> 00:02:09.000
The next attempt gives us fresh evidence of learning.

39
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.068
At home, ask: What can you do now?

40
00:02:12.068 --> 00:02:13.601
What helped you improve?

41
00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:15.519
What will you try next?

42
00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:21.000
And if a gradebook entry needs explaining, please connect with the teacher.

